WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.361 --> 00:00:02.884
A cluster connection is only the
beginning.

2
00:00:03.345 --> 00:00:05.427
In OpenCode, the useful goal is a

3
00:00:05.427 --> 00:00:08.832
repeatable ORCA workflow: submit a small
calculation,

4
00:00:09.273 --> 00:00:11.616
inspect what really happened, and save the

5
00:00:11.616 --> 00:00:13.839
verified procedure as a reusable skill.

6
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.684
The recording starts by checking Slurm,
the

7
00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:19.465
available partitions, and the queue.

8
00:00:19.966 --> 00:00:22.028
This confirms access to the scheduler.

9
00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:24.771
The calculation still needs the right
software

10
00:00:24.771 --> 00:00:26.473
environment on a compute node.

11
00:00:27.054 --> 00:00:28.916
A working connection is one piece of

12
00:00:28.956 --> 00:00:31.579
evidence, not the final scientific result.

13
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.321
Use a small water geometry optimization as

14
00:00:36.321 --> 00:00:40.162
the test. The recorded request specifies
four

15
00:00:40.162 --> 00:00:42.503
processors and eight gigabytes of memory.

16
00:00:42.943 --> 00:00:44.944
Match the ORCA process count to the

17
00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:48.165
Slurm allocation, and budget memory per
process

18
00:00:48.165 --> 00:00:51.906
with headroom. Keep the calculation
method, basis,

19
00:00:51.926 --> 00:00:54.106
charge, and input geometry explicit.

20
00:00:55.187 --> 00:00:57.867
The first attempts fail during startup.

21
00:00:58.088 --> 00:01:01.629
OpenCode traces the problem through module
availability,

22
00:01:01.909 --> 00:01:04.710
missing MPI libraries, and a launcher
wrapper

23
00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.570
that shadows the intended program.

24
00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.231
These fixes belong to this recorded
cluster

25
00:01:09.231 --> 00:01:12.192
environment. Recheck current modules and
paths before

26
00:01:12.192 --> 00:01:13.452
reusing them elsewhere.

27
00:01:13.692 --> 00:01:16.573
Preserve the scientific input while
repairing execution.

28
00:01:17.793 --> 00:01:19.935
A submitted job is not yet a

29
00:01:19.935 --> 00:01:23.357
successful calculation. Read the ORCA
output and

30
00:01:23.357 --> 00:01:24.217
the error log.

31
00:01:24.597 --> 00:01:28.980
Require normal termination and the
expected optimization-completion

32
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.621
marker, then inspect the final energy and

33
00:01:31.621 --> 00:01:34.943
geometry. The recording reports a
completed water

34
00:01:34.943 --> 00:01:38.765
optimization. That supports this test
case; broader

35
00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.186
chemistry still needs its own validation.

36
00:01:42.487 --> 00:01:45.068
Now convert the tested workflow into a

37
00:01:45.068 --> 00:01:48.610
skill document. The recording shows an
orca-slurm

38
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.452
folder containing SKILL dot M D, a

39
00:01:51.452 --> 00:01:54.694
batch-script template, a water input, and
reference

40
00:01:54.694 --> 00:01:58.156
notes. Store the preflight checks,
resource settings,

41
00:01:58.336 --> 00:02:01.317
verification rules, and known failures
together.

42
00:02:01.818 --> 00:02:04.219
The next task can reuse an inspectable

43
00:02:04.219 --> 00:02:04.819
procedure.

44
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.643
For the next job, ask OpenCode to

45
00:02:08.663 --> 00:02:11.246
use the orca-slurm skill and describe the

46
00:02:11.246 --> 00:02:12.327
calculation you want.

47
00:02:12.527 --> 00:02:14.609
Check that the skill is actually available

48
00:02:14.649 --> 00:02:17.612
and loaded. Its instructions guide the
agent,

49
00:02:17.773 --> 00:02:19.855
while the connected tools perform the
work.

50
00:02:19.995 --> 00:02:22.478
Keep permissions, resource limits, and the
final

51
00:02:22.478 --> 00:02:23.939
verification gate in place.

52
00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.283
When a new failure reveals a useful

53
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:29.725
fix, update the skill with the cause,

54
00:02:29.805 --> 00:02:31.427
the correction, and the evidence.

55
00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:34.730
The improvement comes from maintaining
those instructions.

56
00:02:35.091 --> 00:02:37.173
Keep the original inputs and reports.

57
00:02:37.393 --> 00:02:39.916
Test a small case, verify the outcome,

58
00:02:40.136 --> 00:02:41.958
then preserve what worked for the next

59
00:02:41.958 --> 00:02:42.359
run.
